WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.470 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  six best-selling young adult authors  have teamed up to write a novel  celebrating black love  blackout weaves together six stories of  love and friendship  set against a massive power outage in  new york city  first on cbs this morning we invited the  six writers to come together in  conversation  they are danielle clayton author of the  bells series  tiffany d jackson author of monday's not  coming  nick stone is author of dear martin  angie thomas  is author of the hate u give ashley  woodfolk is author of the fly girls  series  and nicola yoon is author of  instructions for dancing  they gathered in harlem to discuss  collaborating during the pandemic  pandemic and the importance of  representing young people of color  in joyful love stories  i'm so excited to be here with all of  you beautiful  ladies thank you let's start with  danielle why don't you tell the story  how you roped all of us into doing  this book with you  [Music]  it's so funny how we all came together i  have a wonderful niece  she is 15 years old and she said to me  why don't the black girls  ever get big love stories why are they  always  the sidekick and i thought you're right  this is a problem  then the pandemic started and on tv  we kept seeing black people being killed  at the hands of police violence and it  was super super depressing and upsetting  and i thought  we have to have some hope and then i was  like okay  i'm just going to rope all of the people  that i love into writing a novel  together  i mean lovingly  i feel really proud about what we made  and i think that when people read it  they will see all of that love and all  of that light  which is what we needed right when we  were going through this pandemic  yeah and usually writing is like very  isolating yes yeah  yeah yeah yeah you know but this was  like this was the best group project  i've ever been a part of  yes absolutely um honestly it was like  super organic like danielle basically  was like  you're gonna do this trope you're gonna  do this trope and as  we put them together we  interwove them with characters yeah and  tiffany's  story which like really connects them  all right  that's the only way i could see this  happening i can only visualize  this book as a love actually as a  valentine's day i have so much of a  connection here  that i'm so excited to see other people  fall in love with my city and fall in  love in my city  and then we all were able to connect it  through our different characters and  different neighborhoods different  neighborhoods and stuff you really get  like the full breadth  of new york city just from tiffany's  story  and then the way our stories sort of pop  in as you get  to each act of her story not every story  in this book is a traditional love story  how do you feel like the story  kind of ties into the larger theme of  love oh yeah i try to do that in my  books show  different kinds of love because it's not  just romantic love that's great  but family love and self self love  that's a huge one for me and my stories  i think  one thing that blackout does and it does  beautifully  is that it affirms to black kids that  you can be the center of the love story  and we're telling them over and over  again that their lives matter but we're  telling them in the context of trauma  and i think that  by showing various kinds of love stories  it really brings that message home that  your lives matter  your dreams matter your love matters  your hope matters all of it  all of it matters we even got a love  story where she get to choose between  two dudes  because black kids have love triangles  as well right  yeah i've honestly never seen that until  i read  angie's story i haven't either and i  thought about that you see it in y.a all  the time the girl trying to choose  between two guys  you know that was that was twilight that  was the hunger games but we don't see it  with the black girls  yeah we don't and you did that you did  that so there are not  just one but two stories that have lgbtq  plus characters in it talk about the  importance of the literary  representation for members of the  community and especially since this is  like pride month  it's detrimental to a person's identity  to never see themselves reflected in  in a positive way so for me it's vital  to tell as many types of stories of love  stories  as possible i feel the same way i'll be  honest i think that if i had read a book  that had a queer black girl in it when i  was 15  i would have recognized the signs  the signs were everywhere and of course  like the six of us can't write to into  every single experience or every single  identity  we you know wanted to make it as  inclusive as possible  like that is why those stories are there  because we  were those kids and they deserve a great  love story just as much as everybody  else  recognizing the humanity in  all different ways of existing  yeah will just make all of us kinder  i think that if any of our readers tune  in to this interview and they look at  this and they're like  i read her book i read her book they're  all so different from one another  you know tiffany she destroys us with  her books  with her thrillers and nicola she mends  our hearts with her  love so what would you say that  you bring to blackout from your previous  work  oh i write boys  i grew up surrounded by boys and there's  something so like rich and beautiful  about black male teens  that we don't get to see yeah so like  that's what i brought i brought  my boy character i feel like i brought  if you don't know i can tell you  the feels yes yes you didn't murder  anybody  i know i'm not proud of you i feel like  i brought a change of format  i wanted to make sure that like you know  this this story had to be fired we had  to throw everything yeah  i feel like you also brought tension  which is something that you do that's a  theme  bringing suspense to a love story look  at you looking so proud  she's so proud right now i really like  to write about the power of a big love  right  to change your life so i think that's  what i bring people  knock it all the time and they knock  romantic stories and makes me so mad  yeah  because love is all there is yeah you  love your work you love your art  you love your friends love is the thing  you also brought  uh yelling at everyone to add more  kissing  you sure did yes i think we're like the  captain planet  all of our forces combined has now  created this wonderful experience  that as we move through the city and  move through the stories and meet the  characters  it comes alive wow i love that  conversation  yeah it was like we were sitting there  listening in and i think even as they  talk about the power of showing these  types of characters  there's so much power in just seeing a  piece like that and being able to feel  the nuances of how they sort of came to  uh to be and how this book came to be  well i also i love  i love the energy of all the different  perspectives coming together and the  respect they showed for  each other which was great to see i  really felt like they forgot the cameras  were there yeah yeah  we love when that happened yeah that's  when the magic happens blackout is on  sale now and you can watch an extended  version of the author's discussion on  our website that's cbsthismorning.com

